3.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A

The NI 45-106 s. 1.1 definition (Ontario, Jan 2021) permits an entity to qualify either by showing ≥ $5 M net assets on its own financial statements or by satisfying the “every owner of an interest is an AI” test. B meets the spousal-income test (> $300 k combined for the two prior years plus continuation expectation). C meets the spousal net-assets test because “at least $5 M” is satisfied by exactly $5 M after the $1 M liability deduction. D’s corporation meets the stand-alone entity test on its $10 M capital contribution. E’s corporation meets the entity test on its reported $5.2 M net assets.  

A is the only structure that fails both routes. Its limited partnership states no entity-level net assets, so the first test is unavailable. The second test also fails: the three limited partners are individually AIs, but the sole general partner is a separate Ontario holding company whose share ownership is never shown to consist entirely of AIs (the “gifts” of cash establish neither ownership nor AI status for the GP). Consequently A cannot rely on any paragraph of the definition.}

